; `String` is a representation, not a handle: a declared `String`
; result over an `Int` body is AX3004, and so is the reverse.
;
; `tyIsReprScalar` named `Bool`, `Float` and `Foreign` - the types
; that "cannot be punned" with `Int` - and not `String`, though the
; String/Int fiat was deleted on 2026-08-15 and a `String` has been a
; header pointer ever since. So `bad` below checked OK, built, and
; died of SIGSEGV with 12345 as its header (the QA sweep's second
; critical finding, 2026-08-18). No struct, no generic, no `cast` was
; needed to reach it.
;
;   line 19: an `Int` body under a `String` signature
;   line 22: a `String` body under an `Int` signature
;
; `fine` emits nothing: `(cast String n)` is the explicit escape, and
; what `Str.strWrapOwned` now says at the one place the standard
; library builds a header from raw words.
(:: bad (-> Int String))

(fn (bad n) n)

(:: worse (-> String Int))

(fn (worse s) s)

(:: fine (-> Int String))

(fn (fine n)
  (cast String n)
)

(:: main Int)

(fn (main) 0)
